 ## Background
-Voxel and grid representations emerged as a response to the computational bottlenecks of coordinate-based neural radiance fields (NeRFs). While early NeRFs used dense MLPs to map coordinates to color, they suffered from slow rendering and high memory usage. The introduction of sparse voxel structures (e.g., Neural Sparse Voxel Fields [S3, unofficial]) and hash-encoded grids (e.g., Instant-NGP, referenced in [S2, unofficial]) allowed for efficient spatial indexing and interpolation. This family has become the standard for high-fidelity, real-time, and resource-constrained applications, including robotics navigation [S2, unofficial], scientific imaging like cryo-ET [S1], and dynamic scene reconstruction [S1].
